WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.420
Can a man get better at something every day, and never remember doing it?

2
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.160
One patient did.

3
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:15.850
In 1953, a surgeon removed tissue from the inner part of both temporal lobes of a

4
00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:16.850
young man.

5
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.840
Afterwards, the patient known as H.M.

6
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.680
forgot new events within minutes, as soon as his attention moved on.

7
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.530
Brenda Milner gave him a mirror-drawing task.

8
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.290
He traced between two outlines of a star, seeing his hand only in a mirror.

9
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.470
Across three days of practice, he improved, much as other people do.

10
00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.490
Yet he did not remember having done the task before.

11
00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:46.053
His drawing improved, so something kept a record of the practice.

12
00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:48.573
Why could he not tell anyone about it?

13
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.827
The record showed up only in how well he drew.

14
00:00:57.707 --> 00:00:59.987
It was not something he could recall and report.

15
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.560
So there were two ways of remembering, and the operation badly damaged only one.

16
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.790
Memory you can bring to mind and state is declarative.

17
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.690
It covers events, and facts about the world.

18
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:17.093
H.M. could almost never form new declarative memories.

19
00:01:18.183 --> 00:01:23.803
Memory expressed in performance is nondeclarative: skills, habits, priming and

20
00:01:23.803 --> 00:01:24.803
simple conditioning.

21
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:26.643
He kept his skill learning.

22
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:32.167
Working memory holds a few items for seconds, like a phone number while you dial.

23
00:01:33.207 --> 00:01:34.207
His was normal.

24
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:40.697
Now a worry. Perhaps recall tests are simply harder, and any brain damage hurts hard

25
00:01:40.697 --> 00:01:41.697
tasks first.

26
00:01:42.417 --> 00:01:46.837
What second result would rule out the idea that the failed task was just harder?

27
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.620
A patient with damage somewhere else, who fails the task H.M.

28
00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.960
passed, and passes the task he failed.

29
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.120
Here is the one idea of this lesson.

30
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.660
Memory systems are told apart by crossed failures.

31
00:02:11.783 --> 00:02:17.783
Damage to one structure impairs task A but spares task B. Damage elsewhere does the

32
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:18.783
reverse.

33
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:22.473
Then weakening one shared capacity cannot explain both.

34
00:02:23.293 --> 00:02:26.553
The tasks rely, at least partly, on separable processes.

35
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:30.023
That crossing is a double dissociation.

36
00:02:30.923 --> 00:02:35.243
It shows each structure is needed for its task in that experiment, not that the

37
00:02:35.243 --> 00:02:36.423
memory is stored there.

38
00:02:37.383 --> 00:02:38.663
Needed, not stored.

39
00:02:39.523 --> 00:02:41.903
That is the key to reading every lesion study.
